## 13. Fix the automated coordination loop in existing owners

### Observed failure

Repeatedly changing a blocked goal to active has produced more status turns
without changing dependencies, authority, artifacts or capacity. An agent cannot
turn that status change into permission for a held Source write, duplicate patch,
model execution or legal approval. Broad idle instructions and broad goal
continuations therefore keep colliding.

This is a control-plane integration problem. Resolve it through the existing
supervisor/catalog/dispatch owners, not another coordinator, queue, runner,
resource namespace or decoder backend. The following is a concrete proposed
protocol, not a deployed scheduler change.

### Work-item contract

Bind each existing task/revision to its objective, upstream artifact identities,
acceptance criteria, selected owner, permitted effects, source/model/profile
bindings, resource requirements and current blocker. Keep the existing task ID
and revision; do not fabricate new parallel catalog IDs in this document.

Separate task readiness from dispatch authority. A complete input packet is not
an execution grant. Admission still comes from the existing resource/Source and
semantic owners at the actual operation boundary.

Use explicit states such as waiting_dependency, ready_for_review, ready_for_admission,
running, awaiting_adoption, closed or refused, mapped to actual existing owner
states during implementation. These are logical distinctions, not asserted new
enumerations already supported by the repository.

### Scope every hold

A blocker should identify the exact task/operation, affected effects/resources,
owner of the blocking condition, evidence supporting it, and observable event
that would permit reconsideration. Examples:

- Canonical Source WRITE contention blocks that write; it is not by itself a
  declaration that all mathematical design or previously authorized immutable
  artifact analysis is blocked.
- A Native reservation blocks new operations needing that admission. It does
  not authorize a new namespace, copied grant or competing process.
- An explicit whole-lane idle order blocks that lane until its owner changes
  the order. The dispatcher must not reinterpret it as a mere lock conflict.
- Missing legal review blocks uses requiring that review. Fictional-fixture
  engineering may proceed only through its own already-selected task and remains
  fictional; agents cannot manufacture the missing legal judgment.

Do not infer availability from an empty namespace-visible /proc/locks, stale
state files, elapsed time, or a cooperative lease label. Use the actual existing
owner's admission and closure contracts. Never release another actor's custody.

### Select only ready, authorized, disjoint work

For the existing dispatcher, a task can enter its ordinary admission path only
when dependencies are satisfied by the exact accepted artifacts, the task is
selected within current authority, the owner is appropriate, and no incompatible
effect/custody claim exists. Unknown or conflicting conditions remain blockers.

Among such tasks, prioritize existing critical-path reductions and required
validation. Do not select easier unrelated tasks simply to report activity.
Contract design, pure fixture controls, immutable evidence review, source
integration, model execution and legal adjudication have different effects;
none automatically grants another.

If the selected ready set is empty, emit one precise blocked disposition. Do not
create work, rerun accepted tests, duplicate an owner's patch, or count a status
turn as progress. If the task needs a new scope selection, surface that exact
decision to the authorized coordinator once.

### Make handoffs concrete and adoption explicit

An immutable handoff should include complete artifact locators/hashes, source
epochs, observed execution and custody scope, failures, limits, acceptance checks,
and the exact requested downstream action. Abbreviated hashes and statements of
intent are not sufficient execution inputs.

Producer completion, independent review and canonical adoption are distinct.
An output can await adoption without keeping a Source or Native resource lease
alive merely to preserve queue position. Actual leases still close only through
their original owner's confirmed lifecycle. Unknown cleanup retains custody and
blocks incompatible dispatch.

The existing integration owner should receive the completed artifact rather
than ask every producer to keep polling for a broad GO. At adoption, revalidate
the current source/parent and task revision. Source drift must not silently
retarget an old candidate or invalidate the original receipt's historical claim.

### Resume on relevant events

Reconsider a blocked task on a matching event: dependency artifact accepted,
explicit scope/owner selection changed, required resource admission became
possible under its owner, or a new source/task revision was deliberately chosen.
Record which blocker version the event addresses.

An automatic retry with unchanged blocker/input/task identities is a no-op,
not a new goal-work turn. Coalesce duplicate wakeups and make adoption/dispatch
idempotent under the existing owner. Observation timeout is not process closure
and never justifies replaying a still-owned job.

The app's goal status and existing task selection must be reconciled: setting
active alone does not clear an idle instruction. Either the coordinator selects
a ready scoped task, or the goal remains blocked with the exact exit condition.
Do not keep automatically resuming the same blocked lane to print another status.

### Acceptance checks for the existing supervisor change

Propose these as owning tests when that change is selected, not as a new test
runner to launch now:

1. A narrow held write does not suppress an independently selected effect-free
   task; a whole-lane idle order does suppress it.
2. Conflicting writes/model operations cannot dispatch without their real
   Source/resource admission, including when namespace lock observations are empty.
3. A new immutable dependency receipt wakes only relevant tasks. Duplicate events
   cannot duplicate model execution, review adoption or registration.
4. An unchanged blocker does not generate repeated goal continuations or repeated
   accepted-test runs.
5. A stale parent/source/task revision refuses adoption; no silent retargeting.
6. Unknown cleanup retains the original custody and prevents incompatible work.
7. A producer's artifact remains unadopted until the actual existing integration
   owner accepts it. Its tests do not become legal/model qualification.
8. When every ready task requires an unavailable grant or review, the system
   returns the precise blocker and exit event rather than claiming progress.

### Current application

The Qwen V3/16k integration and fresh-pair provenance already have owners. Keep
them there. Our synthetic30-test candidate is explicitly unadopted and must not
be made an invented prerequisite for their work. No further review from this lane
is currently selected. The concrete coordination proposal is for the existing
dispatcher to register that state once and wake this lane only for a relevant,
explicit disjoint assignment, rather than replaying automatic continue turns.

This proposal provides a path around unnecessary global serialization while
preserving real dependency, resource, source, checkpoint and legal-review gates.
It cannot guarantee a nonempty ready set when those prerequisites genuinely
cover every remaining task.

